---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../../components/Sequence.astro";

const FRAME = "web/app/src/component-frame.tsx";
const CV = "server/src/iki_server/components/views.py";
const BRIDGE = "server/src/iki_server/components/bridge.js";

const H = {
  short: "Where a plugin's own view runs",
  how: "A plugin tab, step by step",
  example: "A plasmid's map, drawn by its plugin in a frame",
  rules: "Modules, frames, the bridge and the tests",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A plugin's web component is one bundled JavaScript module, checked by its SHA-256 when the
      deployment is built and again before it's served. It runs in an <code
        >iframe sandbox="allow-scripts"</code
      > whose document has no one's origin, so it can't read Iki's cookies, storage or pages, or
      call the API as the person. Its policy is <code>connect-src 'none'</code>: the page reads
      for it, and hands it data over a <code>MessageChannel</code> port. A script that runs first
      takes away WebRTC and nested frames, which no policy covers. Whatever the component asks
      for comes back over the port, and the page checks each message itself (<Adr
        n={[84, 102, 117, 170]}
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <Sequence
      id="frame-dg"
      title="Opening /entities/PLS-…/components/iki/plasmid.plasmid_map/"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "page", label: "Page", sub: "ComponentFrame" },
        { id: "server", label: "Server" },
        { id: "frame", label: "Frame", sub: "opaque origin" },
        { id: "el", label: "Plugin element" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { id: "list", from: "page", to: "server", label: "GET /api/v1/components: the frame's URL" },
        { id: "load", from: "page", to: "server", label: "iframe src: /plugin-components/<name>/<digest>/" },
        { id: "served", from: "server", to: "frame", label: "harden script, module (SRI), bridge; strict CSP", dashed: true },
        { id: "connect", from: "page", to: "frame", label: "iki-connect, with one MessageChannel port" },
        { id: "props", from: "frame", to: "el", label: "entity, entities, place" },
        { id: "event", from: "el", to: "frame", label: "iki-select, iki-more, iki-refine, iki-open, iki-run" },
        { id: "check", from: "frame", to: "page", label: "over the port; the page decides" },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="list"><p>The API gives each installed component's frame URL, digest included.</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/components.py" sym='"frame": reverse("component_frame"' /></p></div>
      <div slot="load"><p>The frame is sandboxed with scripts only and no referrer; a frame that hasn't started in 15 seconds shows as failed.</p><p><Path p={FRAME} sym="START_TIMEOUT_MS = 15_000" /></p></div>
      <div slot="served"><p>404 unless the installed component's SHA-256 is the digest in the URL, and the module on disk still has it. The frame and module are cached for a year, since an address never changes.</p><p><Path p={CV} sym='"connect-src": [CSP.NONE]' />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/web/frame_harden.js" /></p></div>
      <div slot="connect"><p>The target origin is <code>"*"</code> because the frame's origin is opaque; the port is what stays private. If the frame loads a second document, the page closes the port and removes it.</p><p><Path p={FRAME} sym="loads.current > 1" /></p></div>
      <div slot="props"><p>The bridge accepts <code>iki-connect</code> only from its parent, then sets the element's properties. Its own comment calls it a convenience, not a guard.</p><p><Path p={BRIDGE} sym="const PROPERTIES" /></p></div>
      <div slot="event"><p>Selections, the next page of what fills its place, a narrower query, an entity to open, and an action its place offers (<Adr n={[117, 170]} />).</p></div>
      <div slot="check"><p>The page acts only on what it gave the frame: it opens an entity it handed over, and runs an action only if the place lists it, by opening the launch page.</p><p><Path p={FRAME} sym="now.place.runs?.includes(message.action)" /></p></div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A scientist opens a plasmid's map tab. The entity route has read the record and the installed
      components (<PageLink to="Pages and routing" hash="example" />). The child route for{" "}
      <code>components/$</code> renders a <code>ComponentFrame</code> for <code
        >iki/plasmid.plasmid_map</code
      >. Inside the frame the plugin's module, bundled from <Path
        p="plugins/plasmid/web/src/plasmid-map.js"
      /> and <code>@ikisci/science</code>'s plasmid map, draws the map from the entity the page
      handed it, with the project's primers it declared it needs:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/plasmid/components/plasmid_map.yaml" start="^entities:" />
    <p>
      Until the plasmid is annotated, the map offers the action that annotates it. If the
      scientist picks it, the frame sends <code>run</code> over the port, the page checks the place
      offers that action, and it opens Iki's launch page.
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/plasmid/components/plasmid_map.yaml" start="^events:" />
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="A component is one module, checked by digest" open>
        <p>
          A component declares its element, its slots and its events, and points to one bundled ES
          module of at most 10 MiB with everything it needs, since the frame loads nothing else
          (<Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/components.py" />). <code>iki deploy build</code> copies the
          module into the plugin's app after checking it against the digest it had when the plugin
          was read; the server checks the digest again when it reads it, and answers 404 if the
          image was changed since (<Adr n="0084" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/components/installed.py"
            sym="class ModuleChanged"
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          Components with source under a plugin's <code>web/</code> are bundled by <Path
            p="scripts/bundle-plugin-components.mjs"
          /> into one self-contained module each, and <code>pnpm test</code> fails on a stale
          bundle (<Adr n="0191" />). The other first-party components are the module as checked in.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The frame's policy">
        <p>
          The frame's page allows its harden script and Iki's bridge by hash, the module by address
          with its hash as the script's integrity, and WebAssembly compilation; nothing else
          runs. It can't connect anywhere, submit a form, or be framed by anyone but Iki. Its styles
          are its own to build (<Path p={CV} sym="def frame(" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          The harden script runs first, as a classic script, before any module: it removes WebRTC,
          whose STUN and TURN traffic no policy covers, and frames of the frame's own, whose globals
          would be fresh (<Adr n="0102" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/web/frame_harden.js" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/web/frames.py" />). Iki's own structure viewer, Mol*, runs
          in the same kind of frame, with the same script first (<Adr n="0085" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/web/viewers.py"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The page checks every message">
        <p>
          The bridge shares the frame with the component's code, so nothing it does is a guard. The
          page reads each message from the port and acts only on what it gave the frame: more of
          its place's entities, a refinement that narrows and never widens, an entity it was handed,
          or an action its place offers (<Path p={FRAME} />, <Path p="web/app/src/components.ts" sym="readMessage" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Exfiltration is tested in three browsers">
        <p>
          An attacker's server, on another origin, records every request and every datagram. Code in
          a component's frame tries every way out a page has, and must get nothing out, in Chromium,
          Firefox and WebKit (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_browser_exfiltration.py"
            sym="def test_a_components_code_gets_nothing_out("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0012" /> says a component fetches its own data with the viewer's permissions.
            Under <Adr n="0084" /> its frame can't connect anywhere; the page fetches for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>@ikisci/component</code>'s <code>Place</code> type has no <code>runs</code>, though <Adr
              n="0170"
            />, the bridge and the app support offering actions (<Path p="web/packages/component/src/index.ts" sym="export interface Place" />).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={FRAME} />: plugin frames on the page</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/components" />: serving frames and modules, and the bridge</li>
        <li><Path p="web/packages/component" />: types and helpers for plugin authors' components</li>
      </ul>
      <p>What a plugin declares besides its components is on <PageLink to="What a plugin declares" />.</p>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0012" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0084" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0085" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0102" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0117" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0170" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0191" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
